          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"sand-la-mare-au-diable-1436be0c77073f88\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Marie’s utterance is principally an explicit avowal that she loves Germain, framed as a reproachful question correcting his mistaken belief that she rejects him. In the immediate proposal scene, the avowal also functions as implicit acceptance of their relationship and therefore has moderate undertaking force. Her sobbing, tears, embrace, and sudden disclosure make the delivery highly emotional but do not independently establish expressive/reflexive core force beyond an intense T/P realization.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The performative force is implicit rather than linguistically explicit: the utterance accepts Germain’s courtship in context, but literally avows love rather than promising marriage or commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Marie directly addresses Germain with « vous n’avez donc pas deviné que je vous aime? » while embracing him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Germain has interpreted Marie’s trembling, hidden face, and tears as repugnance and is about to leave. Marie stops and embraces him, then reveals that these signs arose from reciprocal love rather than rejection. Her rhetorical question both corrects his misunderstanding and supplies the positive response sought in his marriage proposal; the narration subsequently calls her his fiancée and identifies their eventual wedding. The scene is emotionally climactic, but its core force remains an avowal with contextual relational uptake rather than a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« vous n’avez donc pas deviné que je vous aime? »\",\"supports\":\"The embedded affirmative clause explicitly presents Marie’s loving state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marie stops Germain from leaving, surrounds him with both arms, hides her head against his chest, and speaks while sobbing.\",\"supports\":\"The embodied response confirms reciprocal affection and makes the avowal an implicit favorable uptake of Germain’s courtship, supporting moderate P; the delivery alone does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Germain had just asked Marie to marry him and assumed that she could not love him; immediately afterward he places his son in the arms of « sa fiancée ».\",\"supports\":\"The proposal context and ensuing designation of Marie as his fiancée support interpreting her avowal as implicit relational acceptance.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense French « je vous aime » in George Sand’s La Mare au Diable.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted core is a present affirmative first-person love avowal.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the explicit avowal, while P captures its implicit acceptance function in the proposal scene. Emotional delivery can be recorded contextually without adding E, and no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
